Y-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which monk wrote the rule influential in western monasticism?","value":"Benedict of Nursia","group":"Medieval People","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Benedictine Rule organised communal prayer, work and discipline.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0855","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0855","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which battle ended with the capture of Francis I by imperial forces in Italy?","value":"Pavia","group":"Earlymodern Battles","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The defeat intensified Habsburg influence in the Italian Wars.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0856","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0856","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which roof slopes inward from two outer edges towards a central valley?","value":"Butterfly roof","group":"Roof Forms","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its form reverses the profile of a conventional gable.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0857","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0857","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which Italian group presented La Tendenza's arguments around typology and urban history?","value":"The Neo-Rationalists","group":"Architecture Groups","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The label encompasses architects rather than a single formally constituted organisation.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0858","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0858","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which architectural term denotes a vault composed of repeated conical spreads of ribs?","value":"Fan vault","group":"Structure","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It is particularly associated with English Perpendicular Gothic.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0859","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0859","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Whose expedition completed the first circumnavigation after Magellan's death?","value":"Juan Sebastián Elcano","group":"Earlymodern People","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Elcano brought the surviving vessel back to Spain.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0860","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0860","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which Vienna exhibition building bears the motto 'To every age its art, to every art its freedom'?","value":"Secession Building","group":"Industrial Buildings","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Olbrich's building represented the aims of the Vienna Secession.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0861","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0861","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which Chicago commercial building is known for Sullivan's elaborate cast-iron entrance ornament?","value":"Carson Pirie Scott Building","group":"Industrial Buildings","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The ornament contrasts with the regular grid of the upper commercial floors.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0862","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0862","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which English victory during the Hundred Years' War is associated with Henry V?","value":"Agincourt","group":"Medieval Battles","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Terrain and English tactics contributed to the French defeat.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0863","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0863","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which German imperial dynasty included Frederick Barbarossa?","value":"Hohenstaufen","group":"Medieval Dynasties","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its rulers contested authority in Germany and Italy.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0864","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0864","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"What is a drainage divide?","value":"A boundary between river basins","group":"Map Concepts","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Water on opposite sides flows towards different drainage systems.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0865","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0865","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Zimmermann Telegram is associated with which conflict?","value":"First World War","group":"Modern Events","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It contributed to the deterioration of German–American relations.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0866","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0866","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What did Napoleon's Continental System seek to restrict?","value":"Trade with Britain","group":"Revolution Context","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its enforcement created economic and diplomatic tensions across Europe.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0867","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0867","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which city was the capital of the Abbasid caliphate and a major centre of translation?","value":"Baghdad","group":"Medieval Cities","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Scholars worked with Greek, Persian and other intellectual traditions.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0868","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0868","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which battle on the Volga ended with the surrender of a major German army?","value":"Stalingrad","group":"Modern Battles","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The defeat was a crucial turning point on the eastern front.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0869","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0869","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What did the Marshall Plan provide?","value":"Economic assistance for European recovery","group":"Coldwar Context","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It supported reconstruction while reinforcing western political alignment.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0870","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0870","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Sforza family ruled which major northern Italian state?","value":"Duchy of Milan","group":"Italian States","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its strategic position made it important in the Italian Wars.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0872","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0872","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which postwar tendency is associated with exposed materials and forceful structural expression?","value":"Brutalism","group":"Modern Movements","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Concrete is prominent in many examples but is not its sole defining material.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0873","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0873","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which early Christian Roman basilica is particularly associated with the intact fifth-century wooden door?","value":"Santa Sabina","group":"Church Features","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The church preserves important elements of the early basilican tradition.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0876","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0876","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which Renaissance architectural treatise did Alberti write in Latin?","value":"De re aedificatoria","group":"Architectural Books","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It reformulated architectural theory through a humanist engagement with antiquity.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0879","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0879","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What did the Helsinki Final Act link to European security?","value":"Human rights commitments","group":"Coldwar Context","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its principles later supported dissident demands.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0880","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0880","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which Milanese hospital complex commissioned by Francesco Sforza became a university building?","value":"Ca' Granda","group":"Italian Civic Buildings","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its original design organised care through courtyards and repeated wards.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0881","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0881","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which treatise by Vitruvius survives as a major ancient source on architecture?","value":"De architectura","group":"Architectural Books","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its ten books connect building with wider technical and cultural knowledge.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0882","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0882","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"What is a 'topiary' feature?","value":"A plant clipped into a designed shape","group":"Landscape Forms","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Geometric or figurative forms shape the garden's appearance.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0883","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0883","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"What process turns loose sediment into sedimentary rock through compaction and cementation?","value":"Lithification","group":"Earth Processes","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The grains become consolidated into a coherent rock.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0884","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0884","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which architectural term denotes a protective or decorative railing formed with short supports called balusters?","value":"Balustrade","group":"Facade","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It is used along stairs, terraces and balconies.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0885","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0885","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Why did vernacular Bible translation matter to the Reformation?","value":"It widened direct access to scripture","group":"Reformation Context","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Theological debate became linked with language and literacy.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0887","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0887","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What does palaeography study?","value":"Historical handwriting","group":"Historical Sources","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It helps read and date manuscripts.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0889","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0889","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which architectural term denotes the triangular gable above the entablature of a classical temple front?","value":"Pediment","group":"Classical","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its enclosed field is the tympanum.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0890","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0890","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which representation makes parallel lines appear to converge towards vanishing points?","value":"Perspective drawing","group":"Architectural Drawing","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The projection models a particular viewpoint.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0891","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0891","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which Russian tsar emancipated the serfs?","value":"Alexander II","group":"Nineteenth People","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The reform changed legal status but left major economic difficulties.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0892","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0892","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"What is the network of alignment guides used to organise a page called?","value":"Grid","group":"Graphic Design","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It creates consistent relationships between text and images.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0893","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0893","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which river flows through Dresden and Hamburg?","value":"Elbe","group":"European Rivers","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its lower course reaches the North Sea.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0894","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0894","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which architectural term denotes a panel between the top of one storey's window and the bottom of the next?","value":"Spandrel panel","group":"Construction","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It can conceal floor edges in a facade system.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0895","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0895","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"What is the separation of liquids using differences in boiling behaviour called?","value":"Distillation","group":"Chemistry Terms","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Vapour is collected and condensed to separate components.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0896","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0896","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which architectural term denotes a passage or space parallel to a church's main nave?","value":"Aisle","group":"Church","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Columns or piers commonly separate it from the nave.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0897","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0897","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which movement sought political union of German-speaking territories?","value":"German nationalism","group":"Social Movements","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its competing versions differed over borders and the role of Austria.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0898","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0898","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"In which Italian region is the city of L'Aquila?","value":"Abruzzo","group":"Italian Regions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The city lies in the central Apennine region.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0899","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0899","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"In which country is Bellini's Norma set during Roman occupation?","value":"Gaul","group":"Operatic Settings","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The druid priestess faces conflict between private love and public duty.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0900","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0900","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"What is an urban conurbation?","value":"A continuous area of merged settlements","group":"Map Concepts","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Originally separate towns become physically connected through growth.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0901","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0901","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which battle is associated with Charles Martel's defeat of an army from al-Andalus?","value":"Tours-Poitiers","group":"Medieval Battles","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The engagement became central to later interpretations of Frankish power.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0902","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0902","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which Milanese basilica has an atrium and a façade framed by two unequal bell towers?","value":"Sant'Ambrogio","group":"Church Features","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Map record","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilica_of_Sant%27Ambrogio","verificationStatus":"inherited_reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","supportScope":"Building context; complete question claim still pending verification"}],"explanation74":"Its medieval form is a major reference for Lombard Romanesque architecture.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0903","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0903","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"What is the overall adjustment of spacing across a sequence of letters called?","value":"Tracking","group":"Graphic Design","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It differs from pair-specific kerning.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0904","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0904","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which European state ruled the Philippines before the United States?","value":"Spain","group":"Colonial Powers","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Spanish colonial rule left lasting religious and cultural influences.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0905","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0905","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which Spanish city initially monopolised much of the authorised trade with the Americas?","value":"Seville","group":"Mediterranean Trade","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Casa de Contratación regulated aspects of the imperial trading system.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0906","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0906","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which Berlin building by Schinkel presents a long Ionic colonnade to the Lustgarten?","value":"Altes Museum","group":"European Civic Context","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Tapestry record","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Altes_Museum","verificationStatus":"inherited_reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","supportScope":"Building context; complete question claim still pending verification"}],"explanation74":"The museum gives a civic institution the monumental language of antiquity.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0909","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0909","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"What is a large molecule built from repeated smaller units called?","value":"Polymer","group":"Chemistry Terms","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Natural and synthetic examples have widely differing properties.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0910","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0910","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which dark grey sandstone is characteristic of architectural details in Renaissance Florence?","value":"Pietra serena","group":"Stone Materials","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its contrast with pale plaster helps articulate columns and frames.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0911","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0911","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which Athenian building housed the monumental cult statue of Athena Parthenos?","value":"Parthenon","group":"Ancient Buildings","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The temple's religious role accompanied its civic significance for Athens.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0912","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0912","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which Soviet leader took part in the Yalta Conference?","value":"Joseph Stalin","group":"Ww2 People","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The meeting concerned the final stage of the war and the postwar settlement.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0913","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0913","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which fortified Veneto town is known for a near-circular medieval wall circuit?","value":"Cittadella","group":"Italian Urban History","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The defensive perimeter remains a defining element of the town's form.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0914","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0914","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which architectural term denotes the principal entrance court or hall of a traditional Roman house?","value":"Atrium","group":"Urban","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its roof opening could admit light and rainwater.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0915","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0915","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which French cathedral is particularly celebrated for a largely preserved medieval stained-glass ensemble?","value":"Chartres Cathedral","group":"Gothic Buildings","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Map record","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chartres_Cathedral","verificationStatus":"inherited_reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","supportScope":"Building context; complete question claim still pending verification"}],"explanation74":"The windows form a central part of its religious and visual programme.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0916","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0916","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which Roman amphitheatre was built on land formerly occupied by Nero's private lake?","value":"Colosseum","group":"Ancient Buildings","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Flavian project returned part of the imperial estate to public use.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0917","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0917","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which building type provided lodgings and facilities for travelling merchants along trade routes?","value":"Caravanserai","group":"Historic Types","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Courtyards commonly organised accommodation and storage.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0918","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0918","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which banking family commissioned Botticelli and supported intellectual life in fifteenth-century Florence?","value":"Medici","group":"Renaissance Patronage","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Their patronage linked financial and political power with cultural prestige.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0919","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0919","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"A medical study compares a treatment with an inactive substitute. What is the substitute called?","value":"Placebo","group":"Scientific Methods","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It helps distinguish the treatment's effects from expectations and other influences.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0920","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0920","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"Which process joins metal parts by melting the joint area, sometimes with filler?","value":"Welding","group":"Metalwork","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The base materials are fused rather than merely mechanically fastened.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0922","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0922","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which battle began the rapid defeat of the Bohemian revolt in the Thirty Years' War?","value":"White Mountain","group":"Earlymodern Battles","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Habsburg victory reshaped religious and political authority in Bohemia.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0923","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0923","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which Medici villa near Florence uses a temple-front motif on an elevated residential block?","value":"Villa Medici at Poggio a Caiano","group":"Italian Villas","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The design reinterprets classical forms within a country residence.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0924","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0924","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What was the central significance of the 1966 Florence flood for cultural heritage?","value":"Large-scale damage to artworks and books","group":"Italian Social History","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The response advanced international cooperation in conservation.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0926","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0926","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What did the Roman 'limes' mark?","value":"An imperial frontier","group":"Ancient Terms","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Frontier zones could include forts, roads and defensive works.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0928","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0928","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"Which process hardens steel through heating followed by rapid cooling?","value":"Quenching","group":"Metalwork","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Subsequent tempering can reduce brittleness.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0929","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0929","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which floor system transfers loads directly to columns without conventional deep beams?","value":"Flat slab system","group":"Structural Systems","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Local forces around columns require careful structural design.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0930","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0930","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"What is the bending of light when it passes between media of different optical density called?","value":"Refraction","group":"Light Sound","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The change in speed alters the direction except at normal incidence.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0931","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0931","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What was a medieval 'commune' in the Italian urban context?","value":"A self-governing city community","group":"Medieval Terms","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Urban institutions gradually acquired powers previously held by other authorities.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0932","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0932","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"At which battle did Hannibal surround and destroy a much larger Roman army in southern Italy?","value":"Cannae","group":"Ancient Battles","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The double envelopment became a famous example of battlefield tactics.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0933","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0933","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Who introduced reforms traditionally associated with the foundations of Athenian democracy?","value":"Cleisthenes","group":"Greek People","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The reorganisation of citizens into new tribes weakened older aristocratic divisions.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0934","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0934","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Gonzaga court developed in which Italian state?","value":"Duchy of Mantua","group":"Italian States","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The family employed artists including Mantegna and Giulio Romano.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0935","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0935","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"At which battle did Germanic forces destroy three Roman legions under Varus?","value":"Teutoburg Forest","group":"Ancient Battles","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The defeat affected Roman expansion east of the Rhine.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0936","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0936","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"What is the planned relationship of prominence among visual elements called?","value":"Visual hierarchy","group":"Graphic Design","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Size, contrast and placement help guide attention.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0938","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0938","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which Roman fortress developed from the mausoleum of Emperor Hadrian?","value":"Castel Sant'Angelo","group":"Italian Castles","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The ancient tomb acquired defensive and papal functions over time.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0939","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0939","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which arch follows a circular segment shallower than a semicircle?","value":"Segmental arch","group":"Arch Types","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It allows a lower rise for a given span.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0940","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0940","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"Which Italian tendency challenged consumer society through speculative and provocative objects in the 1960s and 1970s?","value":"Radical design","group":"Design Movements","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its projects questioned conventional functionalism and the role of the designer.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0941","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0941","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which hard igneous stone commonly contains quartz, feldspar and mica?","value":"Granite","group":"Stone Materials","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its coarse grains reflect slow cooling beneath the Earth's surface.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0942","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0942","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"In which Hamburg district does the Elbphilharmonie stand above a former warehouse?","value":"HafenCity","group":"Architecture Adaptation","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The new concert hall forms part of a wider waterfront transformation.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0943","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0943","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which dynasty ruled Byzantium during the reign of Basil II?","value":"Macedonian dynasty","group":"Medieval Dynasties","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The period included military expansion and cultural activity.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0944","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0944","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"A cavetto moulding has which profile?","value":"Concave quarter-circle","group":"Classical","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"A cavetto is a concave moulding; a torus is convex.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0945","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0945","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which Siena square is organised as a shell-shaped sloping space before the town hall?","value":"Piazza del Campo","group":"Urban Spaces","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its form accommodates civic gathering and the Palio.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0946","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0946","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which French city was the papal residence for much of the fourteenth century?","value":"Avignon","group":"Medieval Cities","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The papal court's presence changed the city's political and architectural importance.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0948","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0948","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What was a central result of German reunification?","value":"Integration of East Germany into the Federal Republic","group":"Coldwar Context","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The change ended the separate East German state.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0949","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0949","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which Japanese group is associated with Kikutake, Kurokawa and Maki's early proposals?","value":"The Metabolists","group":"Architecture Groups","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Their work explored large frameworks and changing urban components.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0950","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0950","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which First World War battle became associated with the prolonged defence of a French fortress region?","value":"Verdun","group":"Modern Battles","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The battle became a symbol of attrition and national endurance.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0951","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0951","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which structural arrangement uses diagonal members to stabilise a frame?","value":"Braced frame","group":"Structural Systems","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The diagonals resist deformation under horizontal loads.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0953","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0953","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What was the central goal of the suffrage movement?","value":"Expansion of voting rights","group":"Nineteenth Context","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Women's suffrage campaigns challenged exclusion from political citizenship.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0954","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0954","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What did Chartism demand in nineteenth-century Britain?","value":"Political and electoral reform","group":"Nineteenth Context","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its programme included wider male suffrage and changes to parliamentary representation.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0956","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0956","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"What is a ridge of debris deposited by a glacier called?","value":"Moraine","group":"Landforms","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It records the transport and accumulation of material by ice.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0958","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0958","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which battle marked a major French victory over Spanish infantry in 1643?","value":"Rocroi","group":"Earlymodern Battles","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The victory carried military and symbolic importance.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0959","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0959","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which Egyptian ruler promoted the worship of Aten?","value":"Akhenaten","group":"Ancient Rulers","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"His religious programme departed from the traditional cult centred on Amun.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0960","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0960","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which architectural term denotes the horizontal band between the architrave and cornice?","value":"Frieze","group":"Classical","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"In different orders it may be continuous or divided into alternating elements.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0961","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0961","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"What is a simplified three-dimensional representation used to investigate a design called?","value":"Maquette","group":"Design Terms","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The model can explore form, composition or spatial relationships.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0962","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0962","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which pope called for the First Crusade at Clermont?","value":"Urban II","group":"Medieval People","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"His appeal led to expeditions towards the eastern Mediterranean.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0963","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0963","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which architectural term denotes a vertical post dividing a large doorway into two openings?","value":"Trumeau","group":"Church","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It often supports the lintel and may carry a sculpted figure.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0970","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0970","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which structure distributes loads through a thin curved surface?","value":"Shell structure","group":"Structural Systems","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its geometry contributes significantly to its strength and stiffness.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0971","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0971","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which city became a major sixteenth-century financial and commercial centre in the Low Countries?","value":"Antwerp","group":"Mediterranean Trade","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its markets connected European and overseas commerce.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0972","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0972","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What was a major consequence of the dissolution of Yugoslavia?","value":"Formation of several independent states","group":"Postcolonial Context","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The process included destructive wars and displacement.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0973","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0973","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What did the Truth and Reconciliation Commission in South Africa examine?","value":"Abuses under apartheid","group":"Postcolonial Context","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its work sought public acknowledgment of political violence.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0974","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0974","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which architectural term denotes a sculpted female figure used as an architectural support?","value":"Caryatid","group":"Classical","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Caryatids take the structural or visual place of columns.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0976","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0976","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"What property describes a material's capacity to store heat?","value":"Heat capacity","group":"Building Physics","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It helps explain how materials moderate temperature changes.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0977","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0977","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which Soviet avant-garde movement connected design with new social and industrial conditions?","value":"Constructivism","group":"Modern Movements","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its proposals extended across architecture, graphics and objects.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0978","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0978","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which German city was a leading centre of the Hanseatic League?","value":"Lübeck","group":"Mediterranean Trade","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its Baltic position supported the league's trading network.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0979","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0979","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What was a principal purpose of Jesuit colleges?","value":"Education within a Catholic framework","group":"Reformation Context","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Their network became influential across Europe and beyond.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0980","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0980","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which medieval city was the principal destination of the pilgrimage to Saint James?","value":"Santiago de Compostela","group":"Medieval Cities","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"A network of routes connected it to the rest of Europe.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0981","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0981","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which architectural term denotes a passage continuing around the choir or apse of a church?","value":"Ambulatory","group":"Church","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It permits circulation without crossing the central liturgical space.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0982","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0982","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which architectural term denotes a series of arches applied to a wall without actual openings behind them?","value":"Blind arcade","group":"Facade","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its purpose is mainly to articulate the wall surface.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0986","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0986","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which author wrote Reflections on the Revolution in France?","value":"Edmund Burke","group":"Political Thought","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The work became an influential critique of revolutionary political transformation.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0987","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0987","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"Which design approach prioritises an object's intended use in determining its form?","value":"Functionalism","group":"Design Movements","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The relationship between use and form was interpreted differently by different designers.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0989","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0989","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which architectural term denotes a Roman arena enclosed by seating on all sides?","value":"Amphitheatre","group":"Urban","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its plan differs from the semicircular arrangement of a theatre.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0990","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0990","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What does numismatics study?","value":"Coins and related monetary objects","group":"Historical Sources","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Such objects provide evidence about economy, imagery and authority.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0991","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0991","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"What process modifies a rock through heat and pressure without complete melting?","value":"Metamorphism","group":"Earth Processes","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"New minerals and textures may form under changed conditions.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0992","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0992","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which Italian cathedral combines a vast Gothic structure with an exterior of Candoglia marble?","value":"Milan Cathedral","group":"Gothic Buildings","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Map record","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milan_Cathedral","verificationStatus":"inherited_reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","supportScope":"Building context; complete question claim still pending verification"}],"explanation74":"Its construction involved a long sequence of architects and craftsmen.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0993","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0993","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What did the Weimar Republic replace in Germany?","value":"The imperial monarchy","group":"Worldwar Context","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The new republic faced economic and political instability.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0995","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0995","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"What approach keeps products and materials in use through repair, reuse and recovery?","value":"Circular design","group":"Design Terms","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The strategy considers more than the initial manufacture and sale.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0996","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0996","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"What is the downward movement of one tectonic plate beneath another called?","value":"Subduction","group":"Earth Processes","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It occurs at some convergent plate boundaries.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0997","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0997","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which representation keeps parallel lines parallel while showing three dimensions?","value":"Axonometric drawing","group":"Architectural Drawing","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"It allows spatial relationships to be measured or compared systematically.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0998","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-0998","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which fortified residential building type was central to many medieval lordships?","value":"Castle","group":"Historic Types","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Military, administrative and residential functions often overlapped.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-1000","nodeId":"REVIEW99-ARCHED-NEW99-1000","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which group challenged aspects of CIAM's functional urbanism through attention to patterns of association?","value":"Team 10","group":"Architecture Groups","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Its members explored social relationships and urban structure.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0001","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0001","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which Italian river has a large delta south of Venice?","value":"Po","group":"Italy rivers","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Po reaches the Adriatic through a branching delta south of the Venetian lagoon.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0002","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0002","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which river crosses Portugal after rising in central Spain and reaches the Atlantic near Porto?","value":"Douro","group":"Italy rivers","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Douro crosses northern Portugal and reaches the Atlantic at Porto.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0003","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0003","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which river flows through London?","value":"Thames","group":"Italy rivers","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"London developed around the Thames, which flows east to the North Sea.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0004","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0004","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which French upland lies mainly south of the Paris Basin and west of the Rhône valley?","value":"Massif Central","group":"European landscapes","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Massif Central occupies much of south-central France.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0005","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0005","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which Italian region has coastlines on both the Adriatic and Ionian seas?","value":"Apulia","group":"Italy regions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Apulia extends around the heel of the peninsula between the two seas.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0006","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0006","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which European capital lies on an island?","value":"Valletta","group":"Mediterranean geography","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Valletta is on Malta; the other capitals listed are on the European mainland.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0007","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0007","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which lake is shared by Italy and Switzerland?","value":"Lake Maggiore","group":"Italy lakes","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Lake Maggiore extends from northern Italy into the Swiss canton of Ticino.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0008","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0008","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which is Italy's largest lake by surface area?","value":"Lake Garda","group":"Italy lakes","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Garda is the largest Italian lake by surface area; depth is a different measure.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0009","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0009","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which Italian region lies between Liguria and Lazio along the Tyrrhenian side of the peninsula?","value":"Tuscany","group":"Italy regions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Tuscany's coast lies south of Liguria and north of Lazio.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0010","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0010","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"The Apennines run predominantly:","value":"Along the Italian peninsula","group":"European relief","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Apennines form the principal mountain backbone of the Italian peninsula.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0011","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0011","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which European range forms an arc through Slovakia, Poland, Ukraine and Romania?","value":"Carpathians","group":"European relief","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Carpathians form a broad arc across central and eastern Europe.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0012","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0012","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Wind-deposited fine silt forms deposits known as:","value":"Loess","group":"European rivers","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Loess consists predominantly of fine sediment deposited by wind; alluvium is deposited by flowing water.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0013","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0013","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which river connects Basel, Strasbourg and the Rhine-Ruhr region?","value":"Rhine","group":"European rivers","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Rhine is a major northbound European transport corridor.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0014","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0014","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which Italian region contains the cities of Perugia and Assisi?","value":"Umbria","group":"Italy regions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Both cities are in landlocked Umbria in central Italy.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0015","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0015","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which country lies immediately north of Italy across the Brenner Pass?","value":"Austria","group":"European connections","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Brenner Pass links South Tyrol with Austrian Tyrol.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0016","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0016","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Trieste lies near Italy's border with:","value":"Slovenia","group":"Italy borders","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Trieste is at the northeastern edge of Italy beside Slovenia.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0017","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0017","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which small European state lies between Switzerland and Austria?","value":"Liechtenstein","group":"Italy regions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Liechtenstein is landlocked between Switzerland and Austria.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0018","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0018","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"The island of Crete lies in which sea region?","value":"Eastern Mediterranean","group":"Mediterranean geography","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Crete is a Greek island south of the Aegean, in the eastern Mediterranean.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0019","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0019","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which city occupies both sides of the Bosporus?","value":"Istanbul","group":"European waterways","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Istanbul extends across the strait separating its European and Asian districts.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0020","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0020","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"The Suez Canal removes the need for shipping between Europe and Asia to sail around:","value":"Southern Africa","group":"Trade routes","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The canal connects the Mediterranean and Red Sea, bypassing the Cape route.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0021","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0021","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which pair correctly matches an Italian city with its coast?","value":"Ancona — Adriatic","group":"European seas","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Ancona faces the Adriatic; the other cities have been paired with the wrong seas.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0022","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0022","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which capital stands on the Tagus estuary?","value":"Lisbon","group":"European cities","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Tagus reaches the Atlantic beside Lisbon.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0023","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0023","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which city is divided by the Danube into historic Buda and Pest?","value":"Budapest","group":"European cities","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Buda lies mainly west of the Danube and Pest east of it.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0024","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0024","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which river forms much of the boundary between Germany and Poland?","value":"Oder","group":"European cities","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Oder and the Lusatian Neisse form most of the German–Polish border.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0025","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0025","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which two countries share the Iberian Peninsula's largest land area?","value":"Spain and Portugal","group":"European regions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Spain and Portugal occupy most of Iberia; Andorra and Gibraltar also lie on the peninsula.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0026","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0026","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which condition helps explain the summer dryness of Mediterranean climates?","value":"Seasonal dominance of subtropical high pressure","group":"Climate","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Subtropical high-pressure systems tend to suppress summer rainfall around the Mediterranean.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0027","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0027","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"The mistral is especially associated with which French river valley?","value":"Rhône","group":"European climate","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The cold northerly wind is channelled toward the Mediterranean through the Rhône corridor.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0028","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0028","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"The sirocco reaching southern Europe commonly originates over:","value":"North Africa","group":"European climate","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The sirocco brings warm air from the Sahara toward the Mediterranean.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0029","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0029","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which European country has both Atlantic and Mediterranean coasts?","value":"Spain","group":"European coasts","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Spain faces the Mediterranean as well as the Atlantic and Bay of Biscay.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0030","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0030","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which Italian port lies on the Ligurian Sea?","value":"Genoa","group":"Italy ports","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Genoa is the principal historic port of Liguria.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0031","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0031","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"The Po Plain is situated mainly:","value":"South of the Alps","group":"Italy relief","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The plain extends between the Alps and the northern Apennines.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0032","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0032","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which strait separates Corsica from Sardinia?","value":"Strait of Bonifacio","group":"Italy islands","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Strait of Bonifacio lies between southern Corsica and northern Sardinia.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0033","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0033","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which Italian region occupies most of the toe of the peninsula?","value":"Calabria","group":"Italy regions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Calabria forms the southernmost mainland projection toward Sicily.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0034","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0034","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which of these cities is farthest north?","value":"Milan","group":"Spatial comparison","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Milan is in northern Italy; the other listed cities lie farther south.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0035","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0035","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which of these cities is on the Atlantic rather than the Mediterranean coast?","value":"Porto","group":"European coasts","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Porto stands by the Douro estuary on Portugal's Atlantic coast.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0036","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0036","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which country borders both the Adriatic Sea and Italy?","value":"Slovenia","group":"European borders","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Slovenia has a short Adriatic coast and a land border with Italy.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0037","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0037","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which body of water lies west of the Italian peninsula and east of Sardinia?","value":"Tyrrhenian Sea","group":"European seas","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Tyrrhenian occupies the basin between the peninsula, Sardinia and Sicily.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0038","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0038","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which city is the administrative capital of Marche?","value":"Ancona","group":"Italy regions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Ancona is the regional capital; all four distractors are also cities in Marche.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0039","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0039","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which city and region are correctly paired?","value":"Urbino — Marche","group":"Italy regions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Urbino is in Marche; Siena is Tuscan, Mantua Lombard, Lecce Apulian and Ferrara in Emilia-Romagna.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0040","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0040","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which state is entirely surrounded by the city of Rome?","value":"Vatican City","group":"European microstates","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Vatican City is an independent enclave within Rome.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0041","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0041","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which mountain range separates much of European Russia from Asian Russia?","value":"Urals","group":"World regions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Urals conventionally form part of the Europe–Asia boundary.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0042","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0042","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which country contains the cities of Kraków and Gdańsk?","value":"Poland","group":"European cities","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Kraków lies in southern Poland and Gdańsk on its Baltic coast.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0043","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0043","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which European sea has especially low salinity because of substantial freshwater input and limited exchange?","value":"Baltic Sea","group":"Physical geography","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Baltic is a brackish basin with restricted connections to the North Sea.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0044","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0044","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which is the correct west-to-east order of these cities?","value":"Lisbon — Madrid — Rome","group":"Spatial comparison","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Lisbon lies west of Madrid, while Rome lies considerably farther east.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0045","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0045","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which country contains the historic region of Transylvania?","value":"Romania","group":"European regions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Transylvania lies inside the Carpathian arc in Romania.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0046","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0046","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which capital is closest to the site of ancient Carthage?","value":"Tunis","group":"Mediterranean geography","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The archaeological site lies on the outskirts of Tunis.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0047","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0047","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which capital lies on the Nile?","value":"Cairo","group":"World cities","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Nile passes through Cairo before branching into its delta.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0048","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0048","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which of these countries is not crossed by the Equator?","value":"Egypt","group":"World geography","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Egypt lies wholly north of the Equator; all four other countries are crossed by it.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0049","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0049","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"A peninsula is joined to a larger landmass by a narrow neck. That neck is an:","value":"Isthmus","group":"Physical geography","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"An isthmus is a narrow land connection between larger areas of land.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0050","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0050","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which map projection preserves local angles but greatly enlarges high-latitude areas?","value":"Mercator projection","group":"Maps","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Mercator is conformal rather than equal-area; Greenland appears disproportionately large.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0051","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0051","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"A high demographic dependency ratio indicates a relatively large number of:","value":"Children and older people per working-age adult","group":"Demography","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The ratio compares age groups commonly treated as dependent with the working-age population.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0052","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0052","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which population pyramid generally indicates a high proportion of children?","value":"A broad-based pyramid","group":"Demography","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"A broad base represents large young age groups, often associated with high birth rates.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0053","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0053","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"The combination of high inflation and weak economic growth is commonly called:","value":"Stagflation","group":"Economic concepts","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Stagflation combines stagnant economic activity with rising prices.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0054","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0054","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"GDP measures principally:","value":"Production within a country's territory","group":"Economic concepts","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Gross domestic product measures the value of final goods and services produced within an economy over a period.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0055","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0055","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which economic sector includes farming and fishing?","value":"Primary sector","group":"Economic concepts","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The primary sector obtains products directly from natural resources.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0056","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0056","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which sector includes manufacturing?","value":"Secondary sector","group":"Economic concepts","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Manufacturing transforms materials into products and belongs to the secondary sector.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0057","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0057","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which institution issues the Human Development Report?","value":"UNDP","group":"International institutions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The United Nations Development Programme publishes the Human Development Report and associated index.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0058","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0058","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which Rome-based UN agency provides food assistance during humanitarian emergencies?","value":"World Food Programme","group":"International institutions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The WFP organises food assistance and related humanitarian support.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0059","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0059","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"The European Central Bank's primary objective is to maintain:","value":"Price stability","group":"International institutions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The ECB's primary monetary-policy objective is price stability in the euro area.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0060","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0060","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which UN judicial organ may issue advisory opinions at the request of authorised UN bodies?","value":"International Court of Justice","group":"International institutions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Advisory opinions are a distinct part of the ICJ’s role, alongside contentious cases between states.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0061","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0061","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which arrangement links Norway to much of the EU's internal market without making it an EU member?","value":"European Economic Area","group":"European institutions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The EEA extends major internal-market rules to Norway and other participating non-EU states.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0062","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0062","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which UNESCO designation recognises places of outstanding universal cultural or natural value?","value":"World Heritage status","group":"Cultural institutions","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"World Heritage inscription concerns outstanding universal value under the 1972 Convention.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0063","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0063","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"Olivetti's Ivrea industrial community is notable for connecting factory design with:","value":"Housing and social services","group":"Italian industrial design","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Adriano Olivetti's programme linked industrial production with workers' welfare, housing and cultural facilities.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0064","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0064","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"Which material was central to Kartell's early reputation in furniture and household products?","value":"Plastics","group":"Italian industrial design","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Kartell developed industrially produced plastic furniture and household objects.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0065","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0065","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"The Isotype visual-language project sought to communicate statistical information through:","value":"Repeated pictorial symbols","group":"Design institutions","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Otto and Marie Neurath and Gerd Arntz developed a system using pictorial signs to communicate quantities and social information.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0066","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0066","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"The phrase 'less, but better' is associated with which industrial designer?","value":"Dieter Rams","group":"Industrial design principles","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Rams used the phrase to express reduction to essential, well-considered design.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0067","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0067","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"Which company employed Dieter Rams for much of his career?","value":"Braun","group":"Industrial design institutions","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Rams became closely associated with Braun's electrical consumer products.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0068","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0068","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"Which feature of the Barcelona chair particularly distinguishes it from a tubular cantilever chair?","value":"Crossed flat-metal supports","group":"Furniture context","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Barcelona chair uses crossed metal supports rather than the continuous tubular cantilever of chairs such as the B32.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0069","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0069","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"Who designed the Tolomeo lamp with Giancarlo Fassina?","value":"Michele De Lucchi","group":"Furniture structure","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Michele De Lucchi and Giancarlo Fassina designed Tolomeo for Artemide.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0070","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0070","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"What distinguishes a cantilever chair from a conventional four-legged chair?","value":"A seat supported without rear legs","group":"Furniture structure","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The cantilevered seat projects from its support rather than resting on rear legs.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0071","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0071","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"Which label is associated with Achille and Pier Giacomo Castiglioni's Arco lamp?","value":"Flos","group":"Italian lighting","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Flos produced Arco, which combines a heavy marble base with an arched metal stem.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0072","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0072","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"The Memphis group's furniture is especially associated with:","value":"Patterned laminates and bold colours","group":"Design movements","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Memphis challenged functionalist restraint through deliberately expressive surfaces and forms.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0073","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0073","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"Which designer is associated with the phrase 'Super Normal', together with Naoto Fukasawa?","value":"Jasper Morrison","group":"Design ideas","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Morrison and Fukasawa used Super Normal to discuss understated, familiar everyday objects.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0074","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0074","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Lavoisier's explanation of combustion challenged the theory of:","value":"Phlogiston","group":"History of science","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Lavoisier's work connected combustion with oxygen and undermined phlogiston theory.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0075","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0075","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which part of the atmosphere contains most of the ozone layer?","value":"Stratosphere","group":"History of science","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Stratospheric ozone absorbs much of the Sun's harmful ultraviolet radiation.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0076","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0076","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which SI base unit measures luminous intensity?","value":"Candela","group":"History of science","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The candela is the SI base unit of luminous intensity; lumen and lux measure related but different quantities.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0077","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0077","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which instrument is used primarily to observe objects too small for unaided vision?","value":"Microscope","group":"Scientific instruments","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"A microscope magnifies small specimens; a telescope is designed for distant objects.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0078","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0078","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which of these forms of electromagnetic radiation has a wavelength longer than visible light?","value":"Infrared","group":"Materials","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Infrared wavelengths are longer than visible wavelengths; the other listed types have shorter wavelengths.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0079","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0079","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which dynasty supplied the monarchs of unified Italy?","value":"House of Savoy","group":"Italian unification","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Savoy monarchy expanded from the Kingdom of Sardinia to the unified Kingdom of Italy.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0080","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0080","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Kingdom of the Two Sicilies was ruled immediately before unification by which dynasty?","value":"Bourbon","group":"Italian unification","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The southern kingdom was ruled by the Bourbon dynasty until Garibaldi's campaign and annexation.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0081","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0081","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which agreement linked Napoleon III and Cavour before the war against Austria in 1859?","value":"Plombières agreement","group":"Italian unification","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Plombières meeting established the basis for French support against Austria.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0082","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0082","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which conflict immediately preceded the transfer of Venetia to Italy in 1866?","value":"Austro-Prussian War and Third Italian War of Independence","group":"Italian unification","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Prussia's victory over Austria formed the wider setting for Italy's acquisition of Venetia.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0083","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0083","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which king of Sardinia abdicated after defeat at Novara in 1849?","value":"Charles Albert","group":"Italian unification","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Charles Albert abdicated in favour of his son Victor Emmanuel II after the defeat at Novara.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0084","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0084","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which political movement was founded by Giuseppe Mazzini to promote a united republican Italy?","value":"Young Italy","group":"Italian unification","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Mazzini founded Young Italy as a republican nationalist movement in 1831.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0085","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0085","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which Roman road originally connected Rome with Capua before extension toward Brindisi?","value":"Via Appia","group":"Ancient transport","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Appian Way became a major route from Rome to southern Italy.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0086","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0086","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which ancient road linked Rimini and Piacenza?","value":"Via Emilia","group":"Ancient transport","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Via Emilia crossed the plain south of the Po and gave its name to Emilia.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0087","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0087","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"In the Roman Republic, which magistrates were chiefly responsible for state financial administration?","value":"Quaestors","group":"Roman institutions","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Quaestors held financial responsibilities, unlike the judicial role associated with praetors.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0088","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0088","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Twelve Tables are associated with the early codification of:","value":"Roman law","group":"Roman institutions","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Twelve Tables made a body of Roman law publicly accessible in written form.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0089","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0089","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which city was destroyed by Rome at the end of the Third Punic War?","value":"Carthage","group":"Ancient conflicts","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Rome destroyed Carthage in 146 BCE after the final Punic War.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0090","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0090","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Roman tradition of Cincinnatus chiefly exemplifies the surrender of:","value":"Emergency political power","group":"Roman political change","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"In Roman tradition Cincinnatus relinquished the dictatorship after resolving the emergency, returning to private life.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0091","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0091","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Under the Roman Tetrarchy, how many rulers held the senior title Augustus at one time?","value":"Two","group":"Roman political change","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The tetrarchic scheme had two senior Augusti and two junior Caesars.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0092","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0092","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Justinian's general Belisarius reconquered North Africa from the:","value":"Vandals","group":"Late antiquity","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Belisarius defeated the Vandal kingdom in North Africa during Justinian's reconquests.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0093","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0093","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which kingdom made Ravenna its capital under Theodoric?","value":"Ostrogothic kingdom","group":"Early medieval Europe","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Theodoric ruled Italy from Ravenna, where monuments preserve evidence of Ostrogothic patronage.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0094","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0094","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which people established a kingdom in Italy with Pavia as its capital?","value":"Lombards","group":"Early medieval Europe","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Pavia became the capital of the Lombard kingdom in northern Italy.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0095","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0095","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which scholar from York helped organise learning at Charlemagne's court?","value":"Alcuin","group":"Medieval institutions","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Alcuin of York was a major figure in the educational reforms associated with the Carolingian Renaissance.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0096","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0096","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Golden Bull of 1356 regulated chiefly the selection of the:","value":"Holy Roman Emperor","group":"Medieval institutions","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Golden Bull formalised the role of seven prince-electors in electing the king who would become emperor.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0097","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0097","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The humiliation at Canossa involved Henry IV and which pope?","value":"Gregory VII","group":"Medieval politics","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Henry IV sought release from excommunication from Gregory VII at Canossa.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0098","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0098","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which agreement conventionally ended the principal phase of the Investiture Controversy?","value":"Concordat of Worms","group":"Medieval diplomacy","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The 1122 concordat distinguished aspects of spiritual appointment and secular investiture.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0099","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0099","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Ciompi revolt in Florence involved chiefly workers in the:","value":"Wool industry","group":"Medieval Italian cities","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Ciompi were workers in Florence's wool industry who sought political representation in 1378.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0100","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0100","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Pazzi conspiracy sought to overthrow the predominance of which family in Florence?","value":"Medici","group":"Medieval Italian cities","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The conspiracy targeted Lorenzo and Giuliano de' Medici; Giuliano was killed, while Lorenzo survived.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0101","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0101","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The War of Chioggia was fought principally between which maritime rivals?","value":"Venice and Genoa","group":"Mediterranean history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The late fourteenth-century war was a major confrontation between Venice and Genoa.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0102","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0102","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Italian League formed after the Peace of Lodi sought chiefly to preserve a:","value":"Balance among regional powers","group":"Renaissance diplomacy","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The league was intended to sustain the balance among the leading Italian states.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0103","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0103","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which French king was taken prisoner at Pavia?","value":"Francis I","group":"Italian Wars","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Francis I was captured by imperial forces at Pavia in 1525.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0104","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0104","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The imperial sack of Rome in 1527 took place during the pontificate of:","value":"Clement VII","group":"Italian Wars","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Medici pope Clement VII was caught in the conflict between imperial power and its opponents.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0105","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0105","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Council of Trent began during the pontificate of:","value":"Paul III","group":"Religious history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Paul III convened the council in 1545; it continued in several sessions under later popes.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0106","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0106","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Jesuit Matteo Ricci is particularly associated with missions and cultural exchange in:","value":"China","group":"Religious history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Ricci worked in China and used knowledge of language, mathematics and cartography in cultural exchange.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0107","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0107","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"After the revocation of the Edict of Nantes, many Huguenots:","value":"Emigrated from France","group":"Early modern Europe","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The revocation led many French Protestants to leave for other European states and overseas settlements.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0108","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0108","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Pragmatic Sanction issued by Charles VI sought to secure the succession of:","value":"Maria Theresa","group":"Italian Wars","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Charles VI's Pragmatic Sanction aimed to preserve the Habsburg territories and permit female succession, benefiting Maria Theresa.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0109","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0109","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which city was the political centre of the Dutch Republic?","value":"The Hague","group":"Early modern Europe","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The States General met in The Hague; Amsterdam was a major commercial centre.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0110","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0110","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Ottoman capture of Constantinople occurred during the reign of:","value":"Mehmed II","group":"Mediterranean history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Mehmed II captured Constantinople in 1453 and made it an Ottoman imperial centre.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0111","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0111","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which commander led the Holy League fleet at Lepanto?","value":"Don John of Austria","group":"Mediterranean history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Don John of Austria commanded the allied fleet in the battle of 1571.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0112","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0112","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which monarch's break with Rome established royal supremacy over the English Church?","value":"Henry VIII","group":"Religious history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Henry VIII's religious settlement placed the English Church under royal authority.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0113","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0113","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Glorious Revolution brought William and Mary to the throne after the departure of:","value":"James II","group":"British history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"James II fled during the revolution of 1688; William and Mary subsequently accepted the crown.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0114","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0114","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which work by Montesquieu analyses political institutions and the separation of powers?","value":"The Spirit of the Laws","group":"Political thought","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Spirit of the Laws was Montesquieu's major comparative study of laws and political structures.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0115","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0115","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen was written by:","value":"Olympe de Gouges","group":"Political documents","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"De Gouges challenged women's exclusion from the rights proclaimed during the French Revolution.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0116","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0116","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The National Assembly emerged from representatives of which estate in 1789?","value":"Third Estate","group":"French Revolution","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Third Estate deputies took the initiative in claiming to represent the nation.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0117","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0117","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The fall of Robespierre is commonly associated with:","value":"Thermidor","group":"French Revolution","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Thermidorian reaction ended Robespierre's dominance and the central phase of the Terror.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0118","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0118","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Napoleon's coup establishing the Consulate is known as the coup of:","value":"18 Brumaire","group":"Napoleonic Europe","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The coup of 18 Brumaire overthrew the Directory in 1799.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0119","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0119","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which Napoleonic institution centralised regional administration through appointed officials?","value":"Prefectures","group":"Political institutions","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Prefects represented central government in the departments under Napoleon's administrative system.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0120","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0120","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which naval power remained Napoleon's major undefeated maritime opponent?","value":"Britain","group":"Napoleonic Europe","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Britain maintained naval superiority and resisted Napoleon's Continental System.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0121","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0121","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Who became the first chancellor of the Federal Republic of Germany?","value":"Konrad Adenauer","group":"European restoration","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Adenauer was the first chancellor of West Germany, formed in 1949.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0122","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0122","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which statesman is associated with Franco-German reconciliation alongside Briand in the Locarno period?","value":"Gustav Stresemann","group":"Italian political movements","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Stresemann and Briand promoted reconciliation in the 1920s and shared the Nobel Peace Prize in 1926.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0123","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0123","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Dawes Plan addressed the problem of:","value":"German war reparations","group":"Nineteenth-century Europe","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Dawes Plan reorganised German reparations payments after the First World War and facilitated foreign lending.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0124","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0124","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Helvetic Republic was established in the territory of present-day:","value":"Switzerland","group":"National unification","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Helvetic Republic replaced the old Swiss Confederacy under French influence in 1798.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0125","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0125","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Franco-Russian Alliance developed chiefly as a counterweight to:","value":"Germany and Austria-Hungary","group":"European alliances","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The alliance linked France and Russia against the strategic influence of the central European powers.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0126","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0126","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Fashoda incident brought which two colonial powers into confrontation?","value":"Britain and France","group":"First World War","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"British and French forces confronted one another at Fashoda in the Sudan in 1898.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0127","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0127","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which dynasty ruled Austria-Hungary until 1918?","value":"Habsburg-Lorraine","group":"European dynasties","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Habsburg-Lorraine dynasty headed the dual monarchy.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0128","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0128","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Nansen passport was devised chiefly to assist:","value":"Stateless refugees","group":"First World War","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The internationally recognised identity document helped refugees and stateless people travel when they lacked a national passport.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0129","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0129","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which US president promoted the League of Nations in his postwar peace programme?","value":"Woodrow Wilson","group":"International history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Wilson's Fourteen Points included an association of nations, though the United States did not join the League.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0130","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0130","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which territory became a source of conflict between Germany and France after 1871?","value":"Alsace-Lorraine","group":"European borders","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Germany annexed Alsace and part of Lorraine after the Franco-Prussian War.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0131","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0131","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The term 'Roman Question' arose chiefly after which event?","value":"The capture of Rome by Italy","group":"Italian history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The loss of papal temporal rule in Rome created the dispute later settled by the Lateran Pacts.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0132","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0132","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Albertine Statute was originally granted in which state?","value":"Kingdom of Sardinia","group":"Italian history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Charles Albert granted the statute in the Kingdom of Sardinia; it later served as the constitution of unified Italy.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0133","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0133","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which company founded by Enrico Mattei became central to Italy's postwar energy policy?","value":"ENI","group":"Italian social history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Mattei became the founding president of ENI, the state energy group created in 1953.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0134","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0134","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Taiping Rebellion was directed against which Chinese dynasty?","value":"Qing","group":"Twentieth-century history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Taiping movement fought the Qing dynasty in nineteenth-century China.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0135","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0135","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact was concluded between:","value":"Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union","group":"Second World War","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The 1939 non-aggression pact included secret arrangements concerning eastern Europe.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0136","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0136","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which Soviet city endured a prolonged German siege during the Second World War?","value":"Leningrad","group":"Second World War","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The siege of Leningrad caused catastrophic civilian suffering from 1941 to 1944.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0137","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0137","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Atlantic Charter was issued by Roosevelt and:","value":"Winston Churchill","group":"Second World War","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Roosevelt and Churchill set out shared wartime aims in 1941.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0138","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0138","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which political development followed the Berlin blockade and airlift period in 1949?","value":"Formation of two German states","group":"Cold War","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Federal Republic and the German Democratic Republic were established in 1949.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0139","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0139","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which eastern European state did not join the Warsaw Pact?","value":"Yugoslavia","group":"Cold War","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Yugoslavia followed an independent socialist course after its break with Stalin and was outside the pact.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0140","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0140","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Hungarian uprising of 1956 was suppressed by forces of the:","value":"Soviet Union","group":"Cold War","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Soviet intervention ended the uprising and restored a government aligned with Moscow.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0141","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0141","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which Polish trade-union movement was led by Lech Wałęsa?","value":"Solidarity","group":"Cold War","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Solidarity developed from Polish labour activism and challenged communist rule.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0142","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0142","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Charter 77 originated in:","value":"Czechoslovakia","group":"Cold War","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The declaration and civic initiative called for respect for human rights in Czechoslovakia.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0143","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0143","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which agreement created the European Coal and Steel Community?","value":"Treaty of Paris of 1951","group":"European integration","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The six founding states pooled coal and steel production under common institutions.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0144","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0144","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The second treaty signed at Rome in 1957 created:","value":"Euratom","group":"European integration","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Alongside the EEC treaty, the Euratom treaty established cooperation in nuclear energy.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0145","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0145","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Ventotene Manifesto was developed under which circumstances?","value":"Internment under Fascist rule","group":"European political thought","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Spinelli and Rossi developed the federalist manifesto while confined on Ventotene under Fascist rule.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0146","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0146","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"At Manzikert, the Byzantine army was defeated by the:","value":"Seljuk Turks","group":"European integration","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Seljuks defeated the Byzantine emperor Romanos IV at Manzikert, a turning point in the loss of Byzantine control in Anatolia.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0147","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0147","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Toussaint Louverture is associated with the revolution in:","value":"Saint-Domingue","group":"Decolonisation","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Louverture was a leading figure in the revolution in French Saint-Domingue, later independent Haiti.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0148","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0148","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which territory became Bangladesh after separation from Pakistan?","value":"East Pakistan","group":"Decolonisation","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"East Pakistan became independent Bangladesh in 1971 after a liberation war.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0149","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0149","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Apartheid was a system of legally enforced racial segregation in:","value":"South Africa","group":"Twentieth-century history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Apartheid institutionalised racial classification and unequal rights in South Africa.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0150","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0150","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which leader became South Africa's first Black president after the democratic elections of 1994?","value":"Nelson Mandela","group":"Twentieth-century history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Mandela became president after the end of apartheid-era minority rule.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0151","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0151","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Cuban Missile Crisis concerned Soviet missiles placed near which power?","value":"United States","group":"Cold War","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Missiles installed in Cuba precipitated a confrontation with the United States in 1962.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0152","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0152","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which Soviet leader negotiated with Kennedy during the Cuban Missile Crisis?","value":"Nikita Khrushchev","group":"Cold War","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Khrushchev led the Soviet Union during the 1962 crisis.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0153","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0153","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which two states were parties to the British–Irish Agreement accompanying the Good Friday settlement?","value":"United Kingdom and Ireland","group":"Contemporary Europe","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The 1998 settlement included an agreement between the British and Irish governments.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0154","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0154","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Dayton Agreement principally ended the war in:","value":"Bosnia and Herzegovina","group":"Contemporary Europe","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The 1995 agreement provided the settlement ending the Bosnian War.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0155","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0155","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which empire dissolved after the First World War, giving way to the Republic of Turkey in its core territory?","value":"Ottoman Empire","group":"Twentieth-century history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The Turkish republic emerged after the Ottoman collapse and the Turkish War of Independence.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0156","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0156","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which city became the capital of the Turkish republic under Atatürk?","value":"Ankara","group":"Twentieth-century history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The republic established Ankara as its capital rather than retaining Istanbul.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0157","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0157","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Meiji reforms formally ended the rule of which institution?","value":"Tokugawa shogunate","group":"World history","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"The restoration displaced the Tokugawa shogunate and reorganised Japanese government around imperial authority.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0158","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0158","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Allied campaign on Guadalcanal took place in which island group?","value":"Solomon Islands","group":"Second World War","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[],"explanation74":"Guadalcanal is in the Solomon Islands, where the 1942–43 campaign became a major Pacific turning point.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0159","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0159","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Match each building with its location.\nA. Temple of Apollo at Didyma\nB. Temple of Concordia\nC. Parthenon\n1. Athens\n2. Agrigento\n3. Didyma","value":"A–3 · B–2 · C–1","group":"Architectural relationships","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","